VAULTX DEPLOYMENT GUIDE
=======================

This guide deploys the VaultX FastAPI backend in Docker on one AWS Linux server.
The React/Vite project is a Chrome extension. It is built separately and loaded
into Chrome; it is not served as the backend website.

Recommended production shape:

  Chrome extension -> HTTPS reverse proxy -> FastAPI Docker container
                                      -> PostgreSQL (managed or Docker)

The commands below assume Windows PowerShell for local commands and Ubuntu for
AWS commands. Replace every value in angle brackets. Never commit real secrets.


0. SECURITY ACTION REQUIRED BEFORE PUSHING TO GITHUB
-----------------------------------------------------

Backend/.env currently contains values that look like real credentials, including
database, SMTP, encryption, and Google OAuth secrets. Treat them as compromised:

1. Rotate the PostgreSQL password.
2. Revoke and recreate the SMTP/Gmail app password.
3. Create a new Fernet ENCRYPTION_KEY only if the existing encrypted credentials
   can be re-encrypted or are disposable. Existing encrypted data cannot be
   decrypted with a different key.
4. Rotate the Google OAuth secret if Google OAuth is used.
5. Generate a new VAULTX_SECRET_KEY.
6. Remove Backend/.env from Git history if it was ever committed. Removing it
   from the latest commit alone does not remove it from Git history.
7. Keep Backend/.env ignored. Do not paste secret values into this document,
   GitHub, Dockerfiles, or docker-compose.yml committed to the repository.

Generate a new JWT secret locally with:

  python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))"

Generate a new Fernet key with:

  python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"

If the exposed values are production values, rotate them before continuing.


1. VERIFY THE PROJECT LOCALLY
-----------------------------

From the repository root:

  cd Backend
  .\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
  python -m pip install --upgrade pip

The backend does not currently contain a requirements.txt. Create
Backend/requirements.txt with the runtime packages used by the source code:

  alembic
  cryptography
  email-validator
  fastapi
  passlib[bcrypt]
  psycopg2-binary
  python-dotenv
  python-jose[cryptography]
  python-multipart
  sqlalchemy
  uvicorn[standard]

Install and run the backend locally:

  python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
  alembic upgrade head
  python -m uvicorn app.main:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000

In another terminal verify:

  Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/
  Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs

Before deploying, run the extension checks:

  cd ..\Extention
  npm ci
  npm run lint
  npm run build

The extension build must finish with a dist directory.


2. REQUIRED PRODUCTION ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
---------------------------------------------

Create a private file on the AWS server at /opt/vaultx/.env. Use one DATABASE_URL
or all five DB_* variables. DATABASE_URL is preferred for a hosted PostgreSQL
provider such as Neon, Supabase, or RDS.

Required values:

  DATABASE_URL=postgresql://<user>:<password>@<host>:5432/<database>?sslmode=require
  ENCRYPTION_KEY=<stable-Fernet-key>
  VAULTX_SECRET_KEY=<long-random-jwt-secret>
  SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
  SMTP_PORT=465
  SMTP_EMAIL=<sender-email>
  SMTP_PASSWORD=<smtp-app-password>

Optional Google OAuth values, only if the application uses them:

  GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
  GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=<client-secret>

If using a local PostgreSQL container instead of hosted PostgreSQL, use:

  DB_USER=vaultx
  DB_PASSWORD=<strong-password>
  DB_HOST=db
  DB_PORT=5432
  DB_NAME=vaultx

Do not change ENCRYPTION_KEY after encrypted credentials have been stored. Back it
up in a password manager or AWS Secrets Manager. Use chmod 600 on the server file.

The current backend CORS policy accepts Chrome extension origins matching
chrome-extension://.*. The extension still needs the deployed API URL below.


3. ADD THE DOCKER FILES
-----------------------

Create Backend/requirements.txt using the package list in step 1.

Create Backend/Dockerfile:

  FROM python:3.12-slim
  WORKDIR /app
  ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
  COPY requirements.txt .
  RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
  COPY app ./app
  COPY migrations ./migrations
  COPY alembic.ini .
  EXPOSE 8000
  CMD ["sh", "-c", "alembic upgrade head && uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000"]

For a single-server deployment, create docker-compose.yml in the repository root:

  services:
    api:
      build:
        context: ./Backend
      container_name: vaultx-api
      restart: unless-stopped
      env_file:
        - ./Backend/.env
      ports:
        - "8000:8000"
      healthcheck:
        test: ["CMD", "python", "-c", "import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://127.0.0.1:8000/')"]
        interval: 30s
        timeout: 5s
        retries: 3

This Compose file assumes hosted PostgreSQL. Do not expose port 5432 publicly.
For local PostgreSQL, add a db service and change DB_HOST to db:

    db:
      image: postgres:16-alpine
      container_name: vaultx-db
      restart: unless-stopped
      environment:
        POSTGRES_USER: vaultx
        POSTGRES_PASSWORD: <strong-password>
        POSTGRES_DB: vaultx
      volumes:
        - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

  volumes:
    postgres_data:

If using the local db service, put the api service and db service under the same
services block and put volumes at the top level. Managed PostgreSQL is preferable
because a single server is not a database backup strategy.


4. FIX THE EXTENSION API URL BEFORE BUILDING
--------------------------------------------

Extention/src/Services/api.ts now reads VITE_API_URL at build time and falls back
to http://127.0.0.1:8000 for local development. Set the production value before
building the extension:

PowerShell:

  $env:VITE_API_URL = "https://api.<your-domain>"
  npm run build

Linux/macOS:

  VITE_API_URL=https://api.<your-domain> npm run build

Do not use an HTTP API URL for a production extension. If a domain is not ready,
use the server's public IP temporarily, but Chrome and secure authentication are
better served by HTTPS with a domain name.

Then rebuild:

  cd Extention
  npm ci
  npm run lint
  npm run build

The extension package is Extention/dist. In Chrome, open chrome://extensions,
enable Developer mode, choose Load unpacked, and select Extention/dist.


5. TEST DOCKER LOCALLY
----------------------

From the repository root, create a local Backend/.env with safe test values and
then run:

  docker compose build
  docker compose up -d
  docker compose ps
  Invoke-RestMethod http://127.0.0.1:8000/
  docker compose logs api

Stop it with:

  docker compose down

Do not use `docker compose down -v` unless you intentionally want to delete the
local PostgreSQL volume and all data.


6. PUSH THE SAFE PROJECT TO GITHUB
----------------------------------

Confirm .gitignore includes at least:

  Backend/.env
  Backend/venv/
  Backend/__pycache__/
  Extention/node_modules/
  Extention/dist/

Check for secrets before pushing:

  git status
  git grep -n "SMTP_PASSWORD\|DB_PASSWORD\|GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET\|ENCRYPTION_KEY"

If Backend/.env is already tracked, remove it from the index only after rotating
its values, then clean its history using git-filter-repo or GitHub's documented
secret-removal procedure. Do not force-push a rewritten shared branch without
coordination.

Commit the deployment files and push:

  git add Backend/requirements.txt Backend/Dockerfile docker-compose.yml deployment.step
  git commit -m "Add Docker deployment guide"
  git branch -M main
  git remote add origin https://github.com/<account>/<repository>.git
  git push -u origin main

If the remote already exists, omit `git remote add origin`.

Optional GitHub Actions validation can run `npm ci`, `npm run lint`, `npm run
build`, and `docker compose build` on every push. Do not put production secrets in
that workflow unless using GitHub Actions Secrets or an AWS OIDC deployment.


7. AWS EC2 FREE-TIER DEPLOYMENT
--------------------------------

Check the current AWS Free Tier page for your account, region, and instance type.
Free-tier eligibility changes by account age, region, and AWS product rules.
Lightsail and EC2 are not automatically free for every account.

A. Create the server

1. Open EC2 in the AWS Console and launch an Ubuntu LTS instance eligible for
   your account's free tier.
2. Create/download an SSH key pair.
3. Configure the security group:
   - SSH TCP 22: My IP only
   - HTTP TCP 80: 0.0.0.0/0
   - HTTPS TCP 443: 0.0.0.0/0
   - Do not expose TCP 8000 or 5432 publicly.
4. Allocate an Elastic IP only if you understand its billing rules when detached.
5. Point `api.<your-domain>` DNS to the instance public IP.

B. Install Docker and Git over SSH

  ssh -i <key-file>.pem ubuntu@<server-ip>
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install -y ca-certificates curl git
  curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sudo sh
  sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
  exit

Reconnect so the Docker group takes effect, then clone the repository:

  ssh -i <key-file>.pem ubuntu@<server-ip>
  git clone https://github.com/<account>/<repository>.git /opt/vaultx
  cd /opt/vaultx
  sudo mkdir -p /opt/vaultx
  sudo nano /opt/vaultx/Backend/.env
  sudo chmod 600 /opt/vaultx/Backend/.env

Paste the production environment values from step 2, then start the API:

  docker compose up -d --build
  docker compose ps
  docker compose logs -f api

C. Add HTTPS with Caddy

Create a Caddyfile on the server:

  sudo apt-get install -y caddy
  sudo nano /etc/caddy/Caddyfile

Use:

  api.<your-domain> {
      reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
  }

Then apply it:

  sudo systemctl enable --now caddy
  sudo systemctl reload caddy

Caddy obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates automatically when DNS and
ports 80/443 are correct. Test:

  curl https://api.<your-domain>/
  curl https://api.<your-domain>/docs

Rebuild the extension after setting its API URL to this HTTPS address.

D. Updating the EC2 deployment

  cd /opt/vaultx
  git pull --ff-only origin main
  docker compose up -d --build
  docker compose ps
  docker image prune -f

The container startup runs `alembic upgrade head` before Uvicorn starts. Back up
the database before schema changes, and inspect logs if a migration fails.


8. AWS LIGHTSAIL ALTERNATIVE
-----------------------------

Use this instead of EC2 if Lightsail pricing and free-trial eligibility are better
for your account. Do not run both servers for the same API unless you deliberately
configure a load balancer and shared database.

1. Create a Lightsail Linux/Unix Ubuntu instance.
2. Confirm the selected plan is eligible for your account; free trials and free
   periods are account-specific and can expire.
3. Attach a static IP and open only SSH 22, HTTP 80, and HTTPS 443.
4. Connect using the Lightsail browser SSH or your downloaded key.
5. Run the same Docker installation, clone, .env creation, Caddy setup, and
   `docker compose up -d --build` commands from the EC2 section.
6. Point `api.<your-domain>` to the Lightsail static IP.
7. Verify `https://api.<your-domain>/` and install the rebuilt extension.

The application commands are identical after SSH. The AWS console, firewall UI,
static IP rules, and billing/free-tier terms are the main differences.


9. BACKUPS, OPERATIONS, AND ROLLBACK
-------------------------------------

For hosted PostgreSQL, enable provider backups and test a restore. For local
PostgreSQL, schedule `pg_dump` to storage outside the instance; the Docker volume
alone is not a backup.

Useful checks:

  docker compose ps
  docker compose logs --tail=200 api
  docker inspect --format='{{json .State.Health}}' vaultx-api
  curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/

Rollback the application code:

  git log --oneline -5
  git checkout <known-good-commit>
  docker compose up -d --build

Do not roll back blindly after a destructive database migration. Restore the
application and database together according to your backup plan.


10. DEPLOYMENT ACCEPTANCE CHECKLIST
-----------------------------------

[ ] No real secrets are committed to GitHub.
[ ] Database, SMTP, JWT, and Fernet values exist only in the server environment.
[ ] `VAULTX_SECRET_KEY` is set and is not `CHANGE_THIS_LATER`.
[ ] `ENCRYPTION_KEY` is stable and backed up securely.
[ ] `docker compose ps` shows the API running and healthy.
[ ] `alembic upgrade head` completes successfully.
[ ] GET `/` returns the VaultX running message.
[ ] HTTPS works for the API domain.
[ ] The extension Axios URL uses the HTTPS API domain, not 127.0.0.1.
[ ] Registration, login, credential encryption, and password reset email work.
[ ] Database backups and restore instructions are tested.
[ ] AWS security group/firewall does not expose ports 8000 or 5432.
